WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.566
Clauses and Complete Sentences

00:00:02.946 --> 00:00:05.725
Every sentence needs a subject and a main verb.

00:00:06.925 --> 00:00:07.923
How to spot it

00:00:08.323 --> 00:00:11.678
Same words, different punctuation or verb form

00:00:12.228 --> 00:00:15.365
The question says “conventions of Standard English”

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:19.160
It’s testing: where one complete sentence ends

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.155
The method

00:00:21.555 --> 00:00:23.747
Step 1: Find the subject

00:00:24.027 --> 00:00:25.991
Who or what is the sentence about?

00:00:26.541 --> 00:00:28.902
Step 2: Find its main verb

00:00:29.183 --> 00:00:31.840
Words like “stretching” or “to stretch” don’t count.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:35.062
Step 3: Test each side of the blank

00:00:35.342 --> 00:00:37.230
Could it stand alone as a sentence?

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.130
Step 4: Two full sentences need a real break

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:45.119
A period, a semicolon, or comma + FANBOYS.

00:00:45.669 --> 00:00:46.779
In a few words

00:00:46.929 --> 00:00:49.765
Subject. Verb. Can it stand alone?

00:00:50.965 --> 00:00:51.787
The rules

00:00:52.187 --> 00:00:54.410
Subject + main verb = sentence

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.558
Wrong: The storm ending.

00:00:56.838 --> 00:00:58.547
Right: The storm ended.

00:00:59.097 --> 00:01:01.248
-ing and “to” verbs aren’t main verbs

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:04.001
Wrong: The reef stretching for miles.

00:01:04.281 --> 00:01:06.667
Right: The reef stretches for miles.

00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:10.181
Because, although, which make it incomplete

00:01:10.461 --> 00:01:12.129
Wrong: Because it rained.

00:01:12.409 --> 00:01:15.015
Right: Because it rained, we stayed in.

00:01:15.565 --> 00:01:17.957
A comma alone can’t join two sentences

00:01:18.237 --> 00:01:21.186
Wrong: The storm ended, we went outside.

00:01:21.466 --> 00:01:24.641
Right: The storm ended. We went outside.

00:01:25.191 --> 00:01:27.363
No punctuation at all is a run-on

00:01:27.643 --> 00:01:30.282
Wrong: The storm ended we went outside.

00:01:30.562 --> 00:01:33.649
Right: The storm ended; we went outside.

00:01:34.849 --> 00:01:35.889
Let’s try one

00:01:36.289 --> 00:01:43.077
The Atacama Desert in Chile, one of the driest places on Earth, ______ some weather stations there

00:01:43.077 --> 00:01:44.638
have never recorded rain.

00:01:45.292 --> 00:01:48.187
The subject is the Atacama Desert.

00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:50.894
The part between the commas is extra.

00:01:50.894 --> 00:01:54.038
So where’s the main verb? There isn’t one yet.

00:01:54.588 --> 00:01:59.898
Receiving, having received, to receive: none of these can be a main verb.

00:02:00.448 --> 00:02:05.497
Receives is a real main verb, so the sentence is complete. Choice A.

00:02:06.697 --> 00:02:07.693
Common traps

00:02:08.093 --> 00:02:09.265
The long fragment

00:02:09.545 --> 00:02:12.384
Forty words with no main verb is still a fragment.

00:02:12.934 --> 00:02:14.011
The comma splice

00:02:14.291 --> 00:02:16.869
Two full sentences glued with only a comma.

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:20.035
Clauses and Complete Sentences

00:02:20.315 --> 00:02:23.151
Subject. Verb. Can it stand alone?

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:24.474
Spot it

00:02:24.674 --> 00:02:28.328
Same words; punctuation or verb form changes

00:02:28.878 --> 00:02:29.586
Steps

00:02:29.786 --> 00:02:31.449
1. Find the subject

00:02:31.729 --> 00:02:35.371
2. Find its main verb (not -ing or to + verb)

00:02:35.651 --> 00:02:39.025
3. Test each side: can it stand alone?

00:02:39.305 --> 00:02:44.879
4. 2 sentences → period, semicolon, or comma + FANBOYS

00:02:45.429 --> 00:02:46.182
Remember

00:02:46.382 --> 00:02:48.759
Sentence = subject + main verb

00:02:49.039 --> 00:02:51.222
-ing / to + verb ≠ main verb

00:02:51.502 --> 00:02:54.707
because, although, which → can’t stand alone

00:02:54.987 --> 00:02:57.323
Comma alone can’t join 2 sentences

00:02:57.873 --> 00:02:58.478
Trap

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:00.872
A long “sentence” with no main verb

00:03:01.622 --> 00:03:05.316
